<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Mutamid ibn Abbad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1040">1040</a>–<a href="/years/1095">1095</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as al-Mu'tamid ibn Abbad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Mutamid ibn Abbad (1040–1095) was the last Abbadid king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, ruling from 1069 to 1091, and one of the finest poets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-ammar">Ibn Ammar</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, but paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> fell in 1085 he called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, who deposed him in 1091 and exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad.jpg" alt="Al-Mutamid ibn Abbad" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Andalus_Dirham_602105.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
